# Adding questions

Edit `src/data/questionData.ts` only. Types live in `questionUtils.ts`. Do not add a new category unless no existing one fits; a new `type` also needs an icon in `TopicVisual.tsx`.

## Place the note

- Put it in the **matching category**, then the matching **topic** (React architecture ≠ React hooks; Babel ≠ Angular components).
- Add a topic only when the theme is new inside that category.
- Reuse the category id prefix (`re-`, `be-`, `ng-`, `br-`, `te-`, …) and the next unused number. Do not duplicate a prompt that already exists; extend or add a follow-up instead.

## Answer shape

- `answer` is `string[]` bullets (usually **4**). Each bullet is **one or two sentences**: enough to use in an interview, never a paragraph, never a stub.
- `difficulty`: `easy` | `medium` | `hard`. Sort is automatic (easy → hard, then A–Z).
- Match the voice of neighboring notes. A short “Interview: …” line on the last bullet is fine.

## Code

- If an API, config, or transform is easier to show than to describe, **include code**.
- Mark it yourself. Inline: `` `navigator.serviceWorker.register("/sw.js")` ``. Blocks: a fenced string with real newlines:

```ts
'```\nfunction debounce(fn, ms) {\n  let t\n  return (...a) => {\n    clearTimeout(t)\n    t = setTimeout(() => fn(...a), ms)\n  }\n}\n```'
```

- Format the snippet as real code (indentation, quotes, no `…` that would not compile unless the bullet says it is a sketch).

## Checklist

- [ ] Right category and topic
- [ ] Unique `id`, one clear `question`
- [ ] Bullets, not a paragraph; not too thin to understand
- [ ] Code fenced/backticked where it helps
- [ ] `npm run lint` and `npm test` before a PR
